  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1234 AM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThe GFS continues to struggle with the evolution of the upper\npattern, while the ECMWF and Canadian are in good agreement.\nEnsembles are handling things a bit better, and the NBM guidance\nis slowly starting to trend towards the ECMWF/Canadian as well.\n\nSunday through Monday...the upper pattern remains consistent\nwith southwesterly flow with embedded energy moving across\nColorado. This will lead to more rounds of showers and\nthunderstorms across southern Colorado. Southeasterly low level\nflow will keep moisture pooled across the Plains with mid 50s to\nlower 60s dewpoints through Monday. Instability both afternoons\nwill be modest near 1000 j/kg and 0-6km shear around 25 kts.\nPWAT values remain elevated on Sunday, drying slightly on Monday\nas the upper high starts to retrograde west. Thunderstorms will\nbe efficient rainfall producers, with flash flooding remaining a\nrisk on area burn scars. A few stronger storms are possible\nacross the Plains with hail near 1 inch in diameter and wind\ngusts near 60 mph. Focus areas are still in question and will\ndepend on individual wave tracks. Thunderstorm coverage looks to\nbe greatest on Sunday, with slightly less on Monday due to drier\nair.  \n\nOverall, temperatures will be pleasant through Monday, with\nmostly 80s across the Plains, and overnight lows in the upper\n50s to mid 60s. \n\nTuesday through Saturday...continue to favor the ECMWF and\nCanadian solutions for mid to late week. Both models develop a\n598 dm high right across Utah into Colorado beginning Tuesday,\nand strengthening through the week. The NBM guidance seems slow\nto warm temperatures and have a feeling they are 10 degree too\ncool. Would not be surprised if by Thursday and Friday, highs\nare back into the upper 90s to near 100 on the Plains. With the\nupper high in place, drier air will be overhead, with PWAT\nvalues falling to near or less than half an inch. Any convection\nwill be confined to the higher terrain, with lightning and gusty\noutflow winds the main concerns.  \n\nThe GFS does develop an upper high, just much later in the\nforecast period. The GFS drops an upper trough out of the\nPacific Northwest into the Central Plains for mid week, then\nbuilds the high in by next weekend. This upper system would keep\ntemperatures cooler, more in line with the NBM guidance. For\nnow, continuing to discount the GFS solution given the ECMWF and\nCanadian similarities.",
